Dominic Raab: The government cannot apologize for its decision to quarantine Spain.
Dominic Raab defended the government’s sudden decision to reintroduce quarantine measures in Spain.
The ministers announced on Saturday that holidaymakers returning to Spain and its islands after midnight would be required to self-isolate for 14 days if they failed to do so.
Spain has been on the list of safe travel corridors on Downing Street, however, the recent surge in coronavirus cases has raised public concerns about the second wave of the outbreak.
On Sunday, the Foreign Secretary made the case that this measure was a "real-time response" to the sudden reimposition of travel restrictions following a surge in reported infections in the region.
He said that an assessment of the situation had been made the previous afternoon, “we took the decision as soon as possible”.
He added: "Oh, we can't apologize for this.
"We must be able to take quick and decisive actions, particularly in relation to our own territory or Spain, and specific countries; we have to act."
Otherwise, the UK will face another risk of infection, triggering a second wave of outbreaks, and then re-impose a lockdown.
Transport ministers face mandatory quarantine, as do holidaymakers who travel to Spain
The government made the decision after Spain reported 900 new cases per day for two consecutive days.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists were not able to cancel or alter their travel plans in time.
He said, “I know that for those who are in Spain or who have been considering going to Spain, this decision is very unkind, but we must be able to take swift and decisive action to protect Britain”.
"We have made notable progress in reducing the case numbers and preventing a resurgence of the epidemic."
Shadow Health Minister Jonathan Ashworth has condemned the government's handling of the issue as unjustified.
He said the sudden decision at Downing Street left holidaymakers "confused and disappointed."
The Labour MP told Sophy Ridge: "Well, I understand why they made this decision, but they've made a decision like this in the last 24 hours without a chapter".
Holidaymakers in Spain right now are feeling confused and frustrated, and so are those planning to go on holiday to Spain and the Canary Islands, who don’t know whether their employers will accept them being quarantined for two weeks.
"Government is just saying ‘We hope that employers will cooperate.’"
"They made this decision without any sense in the last 24 hours."
According to CDC, salmonella is a bacterial food-borne disease that causes 1.35 million infections each year, 26,500 hospitalizations and 420 deaths.
Common symptoms of salmonella infection include diarrhea, fever, and stomach cramps, which may start showing within six hours to six days of exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms usually last for four to seven days, and most people recover without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
A couple in California’s dog park was attacked during lunchtime because they were not wearing masks (video) - RT TV's US News
First, there is a disgraceful disguise, and then there is a fistfight.
A couple in California encountered this when having lunch with their dog at a dog park – a woman sprayed pepper spray at them because they were not wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley say they were lunching at the Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego with their three-month-old Bassett Hound on Thursday.
When two people take their masks off to eat and one "an old lady that popped out of nowhere" approaches them calling them "idiots" because they don't wear masks while saying, "We're not eating with our faces covered."
O'Brien forces the woman to leave the park and stop her harassing behavior, at which point she sprays pepper spray at both of them.
The video taken by bystanders begins with Kelley fighting for the spray can with a woman, and he ends up spraying himself in the face.
After the attacker leaves with his dog, O'Brien can be heard crying in the background.
O'Brien posted the video on Facebook and told ABC 10 that she wanted the woman to go to jail.
Masks are mandatory throughout California, even where the air is fresh.
People can only remove their masks during designated times, such as when eating.
However, the state ' s new coronary guidelines for disease control do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to enforce this provision.
Even in the current era of “face shaming”, this is an offence punishable by a fine of US$ 1,000 or a prison term of up to three years in a state prison.
The egg-selling Indore boy was knocked over for refusing to bribe a car and got a flat and a free education of Rs. 100 bribes.
On Thursday, Paras Raykar's wagon full of eggs was reportedly overturned by municipal officials in Indore.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who sold his eggs and was hit by a pickup truck overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, the boy who lived in Indore received support from all over the country, helping them recover from the loss of their home and restart their business.
The boy's family also said that politicians including Nationalist Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had reached out to them and offered help as well.
The boy's family also received an apartment as a gift and a promise to cover their children's educational expenses.
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar's cart selling eggs was overturned by Indore municipal officials, as the city implemented a staggered opening from left to right amid the pandemic.
In a video, the boy said that in the morning, officials had warned him that if he did not pay a bribe of Rs. 100, his car would be turned upside down, and that the video was quickly disseminated in social media.
The boy said that when he refused to hand over 100 rupees, the officials had his car overturned and smashed all the eggs in it.
The boy also says in the video that due to the impact of the pandemic, daily sales of eggs have declined, and these losses are an additional blow to him.
Since Thursday, the boy's family has received a large amount of support from across the country, with many providing cash or goodwill assistance.
The locals also extended help to this family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh funded them and helped the children enroll in a college; People's Party MP Ramesh Mendola also assured that a flat would be provided to the boy's family under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Press Club also provided food and money for the boy.
The office of Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also offered assistance.
Paras' grandfather says: "We have received much support from people across the country."
(MP) Ramesh Mendola Assurance Company has provided us with a suite of apartments under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He provided a bicycle and an installment of Rs 2,500.
Digvijaya Singh pledged to fund Rs 10,000 and promised to look after the education of the two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us to offer help.
(A representative from Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal’s office) also called and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also offered support.”
Foreign Secretary Priti Patel demands explanation from Twitter and Instagram over improper content published by Wiley
Because Twitter and Instagram failed to promptly remove inappropriate content published by Wiley, Priti Patel is demanding an explanation from both platforms.
Police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic comments posted on social media by the Grime singer.
The Minister of Home Affairs tweeted on Sunday: "Wiley's anti-Semitic content is deeply abhorrent."
Twitter and Instagram have not removed the content in a timely manner, and I have asked them for a full explanation.
Social media companies must act more swiftly to remove such jaw-dropping hate speech from their platforms."
He criticized Twitter for "ignoring antisemitism" because Wiley's tweets remained visible 12 hours after they were posted.
Since then, several tweets have been removed from the website and the account number of the 41-year-old singer has been blocked for seven days.
Twitter previously stated that it had temporarily suspended Wiley's account "for violating our policy on hate speech." Facebook said that "hate speech is not allowed on Instagram."
Saturday, the Metropolitan Police Department stated that it is “extremely concerned” about all reports of anti-Semitism and that relevant materials were being assessed.
Meanwhile, A List, Wiley’s management company, severed its relationship with the star.
The ex-agent of the singer, John Woolf, who is Jewish, wrote on Twitter: “Given the anti-Semitic content Wiley posted today, we @A_ListMGMT have severed all ties with him. There is no place for anti-Semitism in this society.”
Earlier, he had stated that he neither supported nor condoned Wiley’s comments but would instead engage with him privately and "educate" him.
However, an Instagram video shows that Wiley appears to have severed ties with his management company.
Other lawmakers also criticized Twitter for failing to remove the tweets promptly.
Labour MP Jess Phillips tweeted on Saturday that she had just seen what Wiley had published. “Why should @twitter be allowing such blatant antisemitism and hatred content to persist? It involves all sorts of dangerous ideas – Jews get things you don’t, they’re in control, they think they’re superior, etc. Such stuff is very dangerous; it must be deleted.”
Rep. Neil Coyle added, “His management appears to be faster than @Twitter in responding. I repeat: laws (including the Online Harms Bill) should ensure that social media platforms are not used to spread hate.”
John Mann, an independent adviser to the government on anti-Semitism, said the tweets accurately reflect the need for changes to the law to bring about a reasonable accountability.
Cracking down on anti-Jewish movements requires police to investigate Wiley’s content and call for his social media accounts to be shut down “to prevent another wave of anti-Semitic venom from spewing out.”
Wiley, whose real name is Richard Cowie, is known as the "Grime Music Father" and was awarded an MBE in 2018 for his contributions to music.
Kudlow says next coronavirus aid package would have more stimulus checks and extend moratorium on evictions
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow promised on Sunday that the federal government would extend the moratorium on evictions and distribute another round of stimulus checks according to the next coronavirus relief plan.
The week before Kudlow made the pledge, the White House's payroll tax cuts were rejected by Congress.
The inhabitants of the village of Massart Mallawi did not wish to make statements about the incident, but only offered condolences to the families of the children.
Mr. Khalid Darwish, advisor to the National Council for Childhood and Motherhood, said in October that approximately 93% of Egyptian children between the ages of 1 and 14 have suffered from violence.
He added that prosecution was one of the most important means of eliminating child abuse and stressed the importance of closer cooperation between the Committee and the Public Prosecution Service in that regard.
A study by the National Commission on Women and Children found that children could also suffer violence in places that are supposed to be quite safe, such as their homes, schools, or clubs, where perpetrators could include those who are supposed to care for them – such as parents or teachers.
The AP criticizes White House press secretary Kayleigh McEnany’s “politicized” briefing, but reporters keep asking political questions – RT America's US News
Reuters criticized Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany of the White House for politicizing press briefings more than making them informative, worrying the media, but apparently forgetting that it is the journalists who chose those questions.
The largest US news agency, the Associated Press, published an article on Saturday stating that White House press briefings "may have broken tradition," as McEnany turned the briefings into "brief and high-profile campaigning and attacking the media" for seeking a presidential re-election, while there was "relatively scarce information" about what the government had done.
The last evaluation has to be seen in context; after all, virtually no new information was revealed at the press conference.
What the Associated Press does not mention is that virtually no one asked for any new information at the press conference.
Journalists often use White House press officers to draw attention to themselves, and Jim Acosta of CNN has perfected this tactic.
They go beyond the common antagonistic relationship between the media and the government and repeatedly ask each other questions about traps, only to question President Donald Trump.
In some cases, they choose to become the news themselves, such as when CBS reporter Weijia Jiang made an unambiguous implication in response to President Trump’s retort of “ask China”: “Mr. Xian, why did you address this to me specifically?”
The July 6 conference is a good example of how it is no longer a source of effective information for the American people.
On that morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR’s ratings had dropped because of false reporting on so-called hate crimes against driver Bubba Wallace and his decision to ban display of Confederate flags in races.
Just as expected, reporters one after another asked McEnany questions related to this tweet, trying to get her to publicly admit that Trump supports the Confederate flag.
McEnany did not let reporters get away with it; such an interpretation would damage Trump’s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president has no position on the confederate flag and that his tweets were about NASCAR fans being wrongly portrayed as racists.
The Associated Press said she sounded “frustrated and angry” during the launch.
AP's story echoes with an earlier column by ABC News reporter Jonathan Karl, who is also the chairman of the White House Correspondents' Association, published in The Washington Post.
Karl said that denying reality and using the White House podium for purely political purposes violates public trust.
McEnany assumed the position of press secretary in April and hosted her first briefing on May 1st, displaying her quick wit and combative nature, which made some Trump supporters view her as a hero.
The news secretary position had been vacant for more than a year before her appointment.
Like other aspects of political life in Washington, D.C., the relationship between the White House press secretary and the media in the Trump era has changed dramatically.
This change has come about through both sides, long before McEnany's arrival.
At the White House Correspondents’ Dinner in April 2018, comedian Michelle Wolf subjected former press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders to vicious questioning, even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called it "shocking."
AP’s White House reporter Jill Colvin asked McEnany at her first press conference, “Can you promise never to lie to us from that podium?”
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume, a veteran of more than five decades in journalism and formerly the ABC's chief White House correspondent from 1989 to 1996, says today’s reporters show "aggressive behavior" during press conferences because they look down on Trump.
In this week's Tucker Carlson Tonight show on Fox TV, he said that this was because journalists focused on relativizing with Trump and ignoring the collection of policy news.
"This is not just a game of cat and mouse," Hume said in discussing the media's intended purpose.
Our purpose is not just to bring government officials down from their pedestals but to get information.
California protesters set fire to a courthouse
Oakland (California) – Oakland police said Saturday that a peaceful demonstration in California escalated to a conflict event when protesters set fire to a courthouse and smashed police stations before attacking multiple officers.
The Auckland authorities reported on Twitter that demonstrators smashed windows, daubed graffiti, and fired fireworks and lasers at police officers.
Some posted on Twitter calling for peace, with tweets requesting organizers to "help us provide safe spaces and locations for demonstrators."
Protests began Saturday evening and were organized by organizations such as “Mother’s Wall” (similar to the groups in Portland, Oregon), among others. Meanwhile, protesters clashed with law enforcement officers who were deployed to protect the U.S. courthouse.
Since May 25, when a protest in Minneapolis ended with the death of George Floyd, US President Donald Trump has sent federal police to quell nightly protests.
Initially, protesters held peaceful demonstrations in Auckland, but turned violent at night.
Around 11:30 p.m., police declared the demonstration to be an illegal rally and ordered the crowd to disperse.
According to news reports, a fire broke out at the Alameda County Superior Court, but the fire was brought under control in a short time.
The police said that at one point protesters broke windows and made racial slurs against residents.
Photos posted by relevant departments show broken glass on the windows of the police station and graffiti on the walls.
UN senior official cancels visit to Pakistan due to flight issue
ISLAMBURG (AFP) The incoming president of the United Nations General Assembly announced on Sunday that he had postponed his visit to Pakistan due to technical problems.
Last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir was successfully elected president of the 75th session of the United Nations General Assembly, which was scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he tweeted that plans to visit Pakistan's capital have been postponed.
Pakistan's Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi tweeted in response to Bozkir, saying "We look forward to your swift visit to Pakistan for a constructive and productive visit."
Just a day earlier, Qureshi Singh announced the visit with great enthusiasm, expressing his desire to discuss a series of issues with Bozkir, including Kashmir, which lies between Pakistan and neighbouring India, both of which claim sovereignty over the entire Kashmir region.
Since gaining independence from British rule in 1947, Pakistan and India have fought three wars, of which two involved that disputed region.
The two sides have fired frequently in Kashmir, causing casualties among troops and civilians.
Pakistan hopes that the UN will adopt a resolution allowing people in the Himalayan region to decide whether they want to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for the settlement of the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United gets Champions League spot; Paul Pogba hints at staying with the club.
Paul Pogba, Manchester United star, hinted that he would continue to stay with the club this summer after the Red Devils secured a place in the quarterfinals.
A 2-0 win over Leicester City meant Manchester United ended up in third place ahead of Frank Lampard's Chelsea team.
Leicester City were still 14 points ahead of Manchester United in 12 games, but the Red Devils were on their way to a fourth-place finish.
In the last two years, Pogba has twice asked to leave the club and so fans have predicted that he will depart this summer.
However, the pandemic means that few teams will be spending heavily this summer, and there is no buyer who can afford both the transfer fee and the wages needed to lure Pogba away from Old Trafford.
This description has helped Pogba in the last few weeks, and his performance and body language have improved.
The French player posted to Instagram after his team won 2-0 that he intends to stay with the squad.
While Pogba may simply be celebrating Juventus' return to Champions League qualification, many fans interpret it as a strong hint that he intends to remain at the club to compete with top European players.
The deal has two years left, but the club can extend it for another year, which means he is effectively still serving his second-year contract.
Manchester United are eager to extend Pogba's contract, hoping to clarify his situation before next season.
For Pogba, staying with the team is comfortable, and his arrival of Bruno Fernandes has made him stronger.
The Portuguese midfielder became the core of Old Trafford, and his arrival enabled Pogba to drive straight into deeper midfield positions and also improved his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Watford to relegation from the Premier League.
Some of Watford's players fell to their knees, while others never returned from the field.
The whistle of Watford’s return to the Premier League for its fifth season has barely blown yet, but no one can say that Watford capitulated without a fight when Arsenal needed all their strength in chasing them down.
In fact, this was Watford’s most spirited and dynamic performance over the past five years, as they were just two goals behind Arsenal when their fight-back attempt almost succeeded in retaking the lead.
With just 16 minutes to go in the match, Danny Welbeck's backheel volley was saved by goalkeeper Emiliano Martínez, and had it not been for that save, Welbeck would have earned his third point for Watford, level with Arsenal and secure their place in the Premier League.
But in the end, Watford failed to achieve this, and their caretaker manager Hayden Mullins stated that if their previous form had been as good as it was in this match, he would not have had to exert himself so much.
Throughout the season, Watford has been lagging behind, and owner Pozzo's management of the club has been chaotic.
A failed campaign may not destroy an empire, but the Pozzo family cannot lay the foundations for Watford to secure its return to the Premier League.
Watford's frequent change of managers has now become an inevitable fate.
On rough calculation, the next reshuffle will be the 12th in eight years, but the influx of fresh blood from a change of leadership is limited.
Captain Troy Deeney faces relegation with the team despite still being eligible for the UAE Super League.
Some players might opt to depart as well, for instance striker Ismaïla Sarr, who said that it seemed incredible to play in the Championship again in September, but saving strength is still the top priority for now.
Arsenal manager Mikel Arteta says that it is impossible to predict Arsenal’s chances in the clash, adding that this makes for an exciting encounter.
Arteta also said that Watford's forwards have been "cheating," before switching to the footballing term "struggling to stay up."
Arsenal’s statement is perhaps unsurprising that they want to go on and win – Arsenal already has the lead in both groups – so Watford have to press hard to get back in touch with their opponents.
Watford conceded a penalty just after the interval.
At 40 seconds, Craig Dawson inadvertently knocked Alexandre Lacazette to the ground during a kickoff, which was subsequently penalized after VAR verification lasted about three minutes, with Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang converting the penalty kick.
Watford pressed hard, and Sarr quickly counter-attacked with the ball on the right side, with Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney providing cover as they closed in on the goal.
A former Labour Party member told The Daily Mail that even if Starmer expelled Corbyn from the party, they would still bring legal action.
“I mean, he really, he made the Labour Party competitive again.
He still has a long way to go in politics, but he's got a very impressive start, and he's gotten to the point where a lot of people who left the Labour Party, who are no longer Labour voters, come back to identify with the Labour Party and see him as a serious person.
Blair indeed fears that the Labour Party is still seen as a party that only raises taxes.
He said: "Well, in my experience, if you're a member of the Labour Party, you have to be very careful about taxes, because the public isn't sitting there worrying about whether you're going to raise taxes, they're more likely to be worried about whether you're going to raise taxes, so you have to be careful about that mentality".
COVID-19 will permanently alter the world economy (Opinion)
Just as various security measures became ubiquitous after the 9/11 events, virus screenings may become a part of our lives as well.
It is necessary to invest in the necessary infrastructure to cope with future virus detection.
If SARS-CoV-2 immunity is only temporary, this investment can provide the guarantee for economic activities.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented policies similar to those of Germany's Kurzarbeit subsidy.
According to this policy, both the working hours and wages of workers will be reduced, with the shortfall in wages subsidized by the government.
This step maintains the link between firms and workers, providing the foundation for robust economic recovery.
Improving the implementation of these policies is also important, which need to become a permanent part of economic recovery tools.
Remote work is likely to become more common.
There is evidence that working from home can be just as efficient as working in an office.
However, many companies are still reluctant to embrace the way remote work is done.
Given that remote work has been widely tried and is showing good results, it may well continue.
With e-commerce continuing to grow stronger, telemedicine, video conferencing, online teaching, and fintech are being increasingly adopted. The pandemic crisis has also accelerated the pace of digital transformation.
Companies engaged in transnational supply chains are grappling with shortages and bottlenecks worldwide.
We may see many of these companies relocating their production back to their homelands.
Finance Secretary Fiona Hyslop said that the pandemic had “crippled” all activities.
The restrictions put in place to prevent the spread of Covid-19 have affected sports events such as festivals and Highland Games in Scotland.
Mrs. Hyslop said, “While we have taken an active step, such as the resumption of drive-in movie theaters, many sectors will still be unable to operate for some time.”
We have worked closely and constructively with industry organizers, who have offered some creative options on how to operate safely in response to the challenges they face.
In addition to direct financial support, part of the funding will be used to promote innovation and sustain the long-term recovery of events.”
The Event Industry Advisory Group notes that the sector was among the first to be restricted by lockdowns and will likely also be one of the last to see comprehensive easing of restrictions.
The group’s chairman Peter Duthie also holds an executive position at Glasgow Stock Exchange, where he has indicated that sporting events contribute nearly £5 billion to Scotland per year.
He says that it is necessary for the government to provide additional funding to this industry and has received widespread support from event organizers and numerous supply chain enterprises that have suffered serious economic losses over an extended period of lockdowns.
Shivraj Singh Chouhan is confirmed to have tested positive for coronavirus disease.
Chief Minister of Madhya Pradesh, Shri Shivraj Singh Chouhan confirmed on Saturday that he had tested positive for COVID-19 and said on his Twitter account later in the morning that he was "Fine" and praised the staff who have been working tirelessly at the forefront of the pandemic.
The Chief Minister also reiterated the warning he issued yesterday, urging people to follow the pandemic guidelines, such as social distancing and wearing masks.
"Friends, I'm fine. The frontline warriors risk their lives selflessly and deserve praise. I salute all the frontline warriors nationwide," Chowhan wrote on Twitter from Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal, where he was admitted yesterday.
Maintain a distance of two meters, wash your hands frequently, and wear masks – these are the most effective weapons we have against the coronavirus,” he said, “I hope everyone can use these weapons to protect themselves and their families.”
Chouhan concluded by saying, "All of you, be careful, I hope you're all safe and healthy. That's what I pray to God."
Saturday, Chouhan tweeted that he had tested positive for the virus despite his best efforts to avoid such an occurrence; he warned that even a little bit of carelessness could lead to infection with the coronavirus.
Today early this morning, Chouhan tweeted that even with the threat of infection still present, there was “no need to be afraid,” and urged those exhibiting symptoms to go get tested.
He said, "If you have contracted the virus, there is no need to fear. Do not conceal your symptoms; immediately inform the doctor and start treatment. Timely treatment will help you recover."
There have been nearly 27,000 confirmed cases in Madhya Pradesh, of which 7,600 were active cases and 799 deaths related to the new coronary virus.
India has seen a surge in confirmed cases in the last few weeks, with 45,000 new confirmed cases per day since July 23.
The total number of confirmed cases of COVID-19 is rapidly approaching 1.4 million, with over 467,000 active cases and over 32,000 deaths linked to the virus.
And what could be a more suitable subject for studying rocks on Mars than a rock from Mars itself? So says Professor Caroline Smith, curator of the Meteorite Hall at the Natural History Museum.
Smith is also a member of the science team for the Mars 2020 mission, and he adds that scientists believe the rocks they return came from Mars.
"The tiny bubbles in the meteorite are exactly the same composition of the atmosphere of Mars, so we're sure that this rock comes from there."
It is believed that this Martian meteorite was created about 600,000 to 700,000 years ago on the impact of an asteroid or comet on Mars, with debris scattered into space.
One of the fragments swept through the solar system and eventually landed on Earth.
The meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been kept by the Natural History Museum since then.
Among the instruments aboard the "Perseverance" rover is a high-precision laser spectrometer called Sherloc that will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic molecules capable of indicating past or present life on Mars.
Adds a SAU 008 to ensure that the operation is done with maximum accuracy.
Smith adds that we specially chose this rock because it is chemically compatible with its surroundings and because it is a very hard rock.
"Some of our Martian meteorites were found to be very fragile; but this meteorite is hard like an old shoe."
Upon finding rocks of research value on Mars, "Perseverance" will cache them on the Martian surface.
Then, these rocks will be retrieved by subsequent robotic missions sent to Mars and launched back into Earth for research and analysis.
A man was found shot dead in Mambourin near Werribi
Last night, a man was rushed to hospital after being shot in western Melbourne.
Upon being called to the police station in Mambourin, near Veribi, at about 8 p.m., the police found a man around 20 years old on Yard Street, suspected of multiple gunshot wounds in the lower half.
A man was taken to the hospital for treatment with no life-threatening injuries.
Police said they were investigating the case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I don't dress up on Sundays; I wear loungewear."
How does your Sunday start?
Tripping over a dog, I stumbled down the stairs on my way to make tea for my wife.
After the ferry came down, we drove on the A3 freeway, went back to London to prepare for the workday, and when we got home, the kids went to bed, and I walked the dog back and ate a bowl of cereal.
I was happy to return to London from the Isle of Wight, because I knew that I would not have a home on the Isle of Wight if I hadn't.
Kenyan police in Garissa arrested for shooting incident
Two Kenyan police officers have been arrested in connection with an incident that allegedly resulted in two deaths during a shooting.
The shooting occurred on Saturday in Soko Ng'ombe market in Garissa county, east of the country, where protests were under way against a man suspected of murder.
The two victims were Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenyan police watchdog has launched an investigation into the shooting incident.
The probe includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Aden Duale, a Garissa lawmaker, called the incident "heinous" and condemned "police brutality" in a post on Facebook.
Several activists have also expressed concerns about police enforcement actions during the pandemic.
According to data from Amnesty International, Kenyan police have killed at least 21 people since March for failing to comply with curfew orders and wearing face masks in line with COVID-19 restrictions.
Earlier this month, police fired tear gas in the capital, Nairobi, and arrested several participants in the annual Saba Saba march, a rally that originated 30 years ago in protest against the dictatorship of former President Daniel arap Moi.
According to the front-runner, this year's protests have become even more radical due to police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdown.
Security Measures Enhanced at U.S. Consulate in Chengdu, China
Sunday, security was stepped up outside the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, and American personnel were preparing to leave.
Three moving trucks entered the building, with uniformed police and plainclothes police lined up along both sides of the street, and metal barriers were set up on the roadside.
The editor of China's Global Times published a tweet stating that the United States Consulate General in Chengdu was required to close within 72 hours, i.e. at 10 a.m. (02000 GMT) on Monday, 27 July.
China accuses some personnel of the US consulate in Chengdu of “engaging in activities inconsistent with their identities.”
The spokesman for the Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Wang Wenbin, did not disclose any details, but merely stated that such activities interfered in China ' s internal affairs to the detriment of China ' s security interests.
China demanded on Friday that the US consulate in Chengdu be closed as an retaliation to the US demand to close the Chinese consulate in Houston, Texas.
Washington wants China to close its consular office in Houston "to protect American intellectual property and personal information."
Following the closure of the Chinese Consulate-General in Houston on Friday, a group of individuals resembling American officials broke into the facility through a back door.
Wang Wenbin said on Saturday that this behavior seriously violated international law and bilateral agreements and China will respond, but did not specify any measures.
The relative closure of the consulate further exacerbated tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage and human rights issues.
The pranksters provide wrecking tools, and in the zoo, baboons wave all sorts of implements.
The primates at Newleigh Wildlife Park in Morecambe, Lancashire, are infamous for attacking cars parked inside their enclosure — smashing windscreen wipers or rear-view mirrors.
Some witnesses saw baboons waving knives, screwdrivers, even chain saws at the Wildlife World in North Wales, Merseyside.
Staff is worried that pranksters might arm these annoying primates, who could then pounce on stopped cars and rip windshields or mirrors.
Now, staff are concerned that visitors are giving them lethal weapons as "a joke," which is increasing their aggression, and there have even been reports of these baboons rifling through toolboxes.
A staff member of a园区 told The Sunday Times: "We're not sure whether tourists who wanted to see them attack cars supplied the weapons, or whether they stole them from pickup trucks and cargo vans themselves."
"They do indeed rummage through others' toolboxes and carry them around. One has seen baboons dragging a chain saw."
Another staff member said, "We have seen baboons carrying knives and screwdrivers. I wonder if it's the tourists who give them these."
The baboon attack was visible, and even the mechanics in the north-west of England knew about the zoo.
A repairman in Greater Manchester said that people knew what was going on at the zoo and that two of his customers had been drugged this year.
They said that one minute the children wanted to see the monkeys getting into the cars, and the next minute, the license plates were gone, but such problems have brought good business to repair shops.
Norris Zoo insists it is just as safe as a McDonald’s drive-thru, as long as visitors stay in their cars during a 5-mile wildlife park tour, and reopened on June 15th, implementing safety measures to address the pandemic.
This zoo covers approximately 222.7 hectares and houses animals such as lions, tigers, rhinos, antelopes, and camels, dismissing reports of armed baboons.
A spokesman said that “we believe many of these stories have been exaggerated through word-of-mouth and have signs of embellishment, which sometimes makes it hard to believe what is found in the park.”
In 2012, Hyundai, the car manufacturer, placed its new-generation i30 car in the zoo with 40 monkeys for 10 hours to prove the vehicle's strength sufficient to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United, Chelsea to the Champions League, Bournemouth and Watford to the Premier League
On Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea beat Leicester City on the final day of the Premier League to advance to the Champions League, while Bournemouth and Watford were relegated, ending a five-year top-flight rivalry.
Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard scored as Manchester United won 2-0 over Leicester City to finish third in the league table under Ole Gunnar Solskjaer's charge.
Chelsea beat Wolves 2-0 with goals and an assist from Mason Mount, securing fourth place and overtaking Leicester City (which had been in the top four for much of the season) to claim the final spot in the UEFA Champions League.
Leicester City will participate in the European league, and so will Tottenham, which drew 1-1 with Wolves in a match at Crystal Palace and ultimately advanced due to a better goal difference than Wolves.
The draw for the remaining two promotion places was pushed to the brink of injury time as Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute, then equalized just one minute later, and drew 1-1 with West Ham United.
Captain Jack Grealish scored a goal for Aston Villa, which could be his last match playing for his boyhood club.
Bournemouth defeated Everton 3-1, but finished one point behind Aston Villa.
Watford were relegated as the second-from-last team after losing 3–2 to Arsenal.
Manchester United scored more than 100 goals during the league, a 5-0 win over the relegated Norwich to qualify for the UEFA Champions League, which was also Manchester United's last game in the Premier League for 10-year-old David Silva.
Kevin De Bruyne sets a record by scoring twice and assisting 20 times this season.
Liverpool beat Newcastle United 3-1 and ended its title hopes this season; the club finished with 99 points.
Moreover, Southampton defeated Sheffield United 3-1 and Danny Ings netted his 22nd goal of the season.
Leicester City player Jamie Vardy, who scored the most goals, won the Golden Boot award.
Gwyneth Paltro wants the child to have a good "value."
Gwyneth Paltrow says she tried “not just to hand them fish but teach them how to fish,” and wants her children to have good values.
Paltrow has two children with ex-husband Chris Martin, ages 16 (Apple) and 14 (Moses), and she hopes they will uphold good values rather than rely on their parents' fame.
And I think that's why I think... it's so important to educate kids about their work ethic, to make their values align with your own values, or with their identity, Mom told Rob Lowe on a podcast.
The truth of the matter is that I can point you in the right direction, but if you just try to do things like others, nothing will come of it.
She adds: "As parents, what I can do for my children is teach them right from wrong and what work is like. Indeed, I tried to avoid just teaching them how to fish."
Paltrow has also taken steps to keep children's lives out of the public eye as much as possible and to ensure that their social media accounts remain private.
Given the upcoming elections, the Renewal Party eventually agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz says Pelosi intends to use money to solve the problem and get the United States to shut down
Washington –– Republican Senator Ted Cruz of Texas accused House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and the Democratic majority in the House of pushing for the coronavirus relief package, which he said was aimed at “spending money to fix problems and shut down America.” Negotiations on subsequent measures are ongoing.
Cruz said on Sunday during an interview with "Face the Nation," that the United States is facing two crises simultaneously: the pandemic that has taken more than 600,000 lives worldwide, and an "absolute economic catastrophe."
Cruz criticized Pelosi for coming to his aid before she was interviewed on "Face the Nation."
Cruz said that her objective is to use money as an instrument for solving problems and causing America to shut down.
Well, look at the $3 trillion bill she's trying to push, which is purely about making money, not actually solving the problem. Our goal should be to get Americans back to work and to support their families.
Senate Republicans and the White House last week negotiated the terms of the fourth phase plan to address the pandemic, which is expected to total $1 trillion.
At the end of last week, White House Office Director Mark Meadow and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Congressional Hill for further discussions with Senate staff, and Mnuchin said in an interview with Fox News Channel on Sunday that Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell would reveal some of the details on Monday.
The most pressing issue facing parliamentarians and a key point of contention between Republicans and Democrats is that unemployment benefits have expired and will be cut off this weekend.
The Democrats want to keep the increased $600 grant in the fourth phase of the new coronavirus relief program, while the Republicans are calling for a cut, arguing that some Americans earn more than they earn from unemployment.
Cruz notes that half or more Americans receiving enhanced unemployment benefits now receive more than they earned working. He says he learned from talking to small business owners in Texas that some are having trouble hiring due to this.
He says, “Of course they won’t come back to work, because the federal government is paying them sometimes twice as much as their wages.”
Cruz believes Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill that would ease taxes and regulations for small businesses so Americans can return to work.
He says, “The recovery bill is not about spending trillions of dollars, but pausing payroll taxes, which effectively gives a raise to every working American. This way truly gets people back to work.”
President Trump last week demanded that any future aid package include wage-tax cuts, but Mnuchin said it will not be included in legislation because Republicans do not accept the proposal.
President Trump confirmed that the next bill would not include a payroll-tax cut but aimed to pin the blame on Democrats.
Last week, Cruz objected to the cost of the new proposal, stating on Sunday that he disagreed with the measure proposed by Senate Republicans.
But he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed by the Democratic-controlled House in May, accusing his opponents of deliberately crafting measures that would harm the President’s interests before the November election.
He said: "The only goal of the Bush Democrats is to defeat Donald Trump, and they naively believe that the best way to defeat Donald Trump is to shut down every school in the United States".
PM Narendra Modi pays tribute to Kargil heroes, says Pakistan trying to “stab India from behind”
Today is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, Prime Minister Narendra Modi said in his tribute to the martyrs of the Armed Forces that despite India's efforts to maintain friendly relations, Pakistan is still trying to stick its knife behind India's back.
Prime Minister Modi says in his monthly radio address, "It is Pakistan that has caused this calamity; they devised malign plans to grab India's land and divert its internal conflicts."
He said that Pakistan is shameless in attempting to occupy Indian territory and divert public attention from its own internal upheavals.
At the time, India was trying to mend its relations with Pakistan.
But, as it is said, the essence of evil is to fight with everyone for no reason.
People of this nature only do evil, even if they want to do good for them.”
He says, "That's why Pakistan stabbed India in its back after it worked so hard to shore up their relationship."
He added: “Imagine what it would be like to have your troops fighting on top of high mountains while enemies were entrenched below.” But with our high morale and real power, we won against the mountain-based enemy forces.
The courage of our armed forces enabled India to show great strength in the first battle of Kargil, Prime Minister Modi said, recalling what he had seen during his visit to the battlefield.
Every year, India holds an event to celebrate the victory of Kargil to commemorate the day when the Indian Army recaptured all Indian outposts in Kargil in Ladakh, which were occupied by Pakistani forces 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army announced the successful completion of Operation Vijay.
The war in the frozen highlands of Ladak continued for nearly three months, resulting in the death of more than 500 Indian soldiers.
Earlier this morning, senior officials and army leaders paid their respects to the martyrs at the Amar Jawan Jyoti monument at the National War Memorial, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of the Defence Staff Bipin Rawat, Army Chief M M Naravane, Air Chief Marshal R K S Bhadauria, and Admiral Karambil Singh.
In the decades following the Kargil War, relations between India and Pakistan have been strained by a series of terrorist attacks.
India insists that talks and terrorist attacks cannot happen simultaneously; Pakistan needs to dismantle all terror camps active on its soil.
Lender firms warn SME loan scheme may not achieve its goals.
Small enterprises are often overlooked by large banks, and thus they increasingly turn to online lending institutions to meet their funding needs.
However, Mr Callaghan said that internet lending institutions have no advantage over large banks because they do not have access to the same low-cost funds, and warned that SME guarantee schemes are not popular.
He said: "I think this scheme may not achieve its objective; people phone me with the expectation that I will provide very cheap funding rather than half of the government loan."
“So, when they called us, they were very disappointed that some people had expressed a desire to get a discount and that the cost of our financing had not changed at all.”
Callaghan added that if fintech firms were able to obtain cheaper funding and pass on those funds to small businesses, this would be “a cinch.”
“This is a bit of a dilemma — large banks have access to funds at a lower cost, but they prefer to avoid risk, so they will not actually lend money in the same way, they will not be able to access data, they will not feel safe unless you have a mortgage on your property”, he said, “Unless we can get cheaper money, I think the market has illusions about the popularity of this programme”.
Moula in Melbourne was also involved in the development of the programme, and the CEO, Aris Allegos, stated that 50 per cent of the proceeds of the guarantees implied in the government programme should be passed on to small businesses, but different lending institutions would take a different approach.
He says, "I can understand that others might not have experienced such a cost structure."
It may relate to their capital structure, or whether they lend to which categories of borrowers and how they assess the risk in those categories.
Mr. Allegos declined to disclose how many loans Moula specifically issued under the scheme, but said that the revised scheme's focus reflects the need to make changes to the scheme, thus providing greater flexibility for both borrowers.
However, he is very concerned about the “two-speed economy” that is running and its impact on small businesses.
He said: "For small businesses, the biggest problems that Moula and lenders face are the cliffs that are being talked about and how to manage the cliffs.
“In sum, although demand is growing somewhat, it has not yet returned to pre-COVID levels.”
Prospa, an online lending company listed on the Australian Securities Exchange, also declined to disclose the amount of loans it issued under the scheme, saying it was business-sensitive information, but CEO Greg Moshal said the government's move to expand the scheme was welcomed.
He said, "We support all enhancements to this scheme that allow small businesses to access a wide range of funding products meeting their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products."
Virginia Beach removes monument to the Confederacy
Virginia Beach (Virginia) — On Sunday, Virginia Beach City Council removed a 115-year-old Confederate Memorial from the public park. Two days prior, the city council had unanimously voted to remove it.
According to media reports, workers dismantled the 27-foot (8.2-meter) tall monument on Saturday morning.
The monument stood outside the former Princess Anne County Courthouse in 1905, where slave auctions也曾 took place.
Thursday night, the city council unanimously approved the related demolition work.
The city council listened to the views of dozens of speakers who believed that the monument meant a split for many and a constant reminder of the sad past.
Given recent protests and vandalism at other monuments, this one has been completely covered up, with locked gates surrounding it.
The monument will be temporarily preserved.
The city now needs to wait for a response from historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to deal with the monument.
Protests continue; police try new low-lethal tools
In Portland and across the country, violent clashes continue between law enforcement and protesters, with debates raging about how to reduce police use of lethal force.
Some companies are ramping up efforts to provide new high-tech tools like police remote handcuffs, as alternatives to more dangerous weapons such as pepper spray, rubber bullets, and flash bangs.
Amnesty International analyzed 500 videos from protests from May 26 to June 5 and found instances of excessive use of force in 125 cases across 40 states and the District of Columbia.
Brian Castner, an Arms Investigation Officer from International Crisis Group, said, "We saw cases of disproportionate use of tear gas, pepper spray, pepper balls, and 40mm impact rounds, which are used in inappropriate combinations of less-lethal weapons."
A portion of people still persist in this view that having selective enforcement is important for avoiding use of firearms.
Corey Pegues from former New York Police Department’s Deputy Inspector stated that we ought to provide various personal protective equipment to policemen rather than using lethal weapons like bullets because policeman is the only profession in America where citizens can use their rights to take human life.
There is a new tool called Bola Wrap, a handheld device that launches an 8-foot Kevlar rope to wrap around a suspect.
Other new types of equipment include long-range acoustic devices (also known as acoustic cannons), directed energy weapons that use lasers to heat human skin, new artillery shells made from materials like foam and chalk powder, teargas, and tasers.
Days after George Floyd’s killing by police in May, protests turned violent and Axon, the maker of the taser that killed him, saw its stock price surge more than 18%, reaching an all-time high.
The market for low-kill weapons is expected to reach $11.85 billion by 2023.
Watch the video to learn about the new high-tech non-lethal tools used by police, how to regulate their use, and what risks these tools may pose to future policing and communities.
UN says thousands of Pakistan-based insurgent militants are stationed in Afghanistan
The United Nations reports that more than 6,000 Pakistani insurgents are hiding in Afghanistan, most of them illegal Pakistani Taliban groups responsible for attacking Pakistani military and civilian targets.
According to the report released this week, the group known as Tehreek-e-Taliban (TTP) is linked to the Afghan branch of the Islamic State (IS).
Some TTP members have even joined IS affiliated organizations with a base in eastern Afghanistan.
On Sunday, the Afghan government did not respond to an Associated Press request for comment on related issues.
It was reported that IS in Afghanistan (i.e., in the province of Horasan) was hit hard by Afghan security forces and United States and NATO forces, and in some cases by the Afghan Taliban.
The report was prepared by the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team, which tracks terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that ISIS’s ranks in Afghanistan number around 2,200 members, although its leadership has been weakened, the group still considers a Syrian citizen, Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani, one of its leaders.
The report mentions that members of the monitoring team were reportedly informed that two senior al-Qaeda commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, had arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
Although the territory occupied by ISIS is shrinking, they are still capable of carrying out some scale of attacks in various parts of the country, including Kabul, the report said.
The group also wants to win over Taliban fighters who oppose signing an agreement with the United States, referring to the peace deal signed between the US and the Taliban in February.
The objective of this agreement is to allow the US to end its 19-year war in Afghanistan, and to hope that the Taliban will guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
The agreement is also expected to ensure that the Taliban are fully engaged in the fight against IS.
The second — and perhaps the most important — part of the agreement calls for talks between the political leaders of the Taliban and Kabul.
Later on Saturday, the U.S. State Department issued a statement saying that its peace envoy Zalmay Khalilzad was still busy in the region, trying to push forward negotiations that had been repeatedly postponed due to disputes over the two sides' plans to release prisoners.
This US-backed agreement calls for the Afghan government to release 5,000 Taliban prisoners in exchange for Taliban releases of 1,000 government and military personnel as a goodwill gesture prior to negotiations.
Currently, the Afghan government is refusing to release approximately 600 Taliban prisoners, labeling them as high-profile criminals, but is proposing to release the remaining prisoners.
The Taliban refused the government's proposal.
"This is an opportunity for both sides to come closest yet to engaging in intra-Afghan negotiations and a critical step toward ending the 40-year-long Afghan war," the U.S. State Department said.
While significant progress has been made in the exchange of prisoners, further efforts are still needed to resolve the issue.
Earlier this week, the Taliban’s political spokesman said that the Taliban plan to hold talks with Kabul’s political leaders after Eid al-Adha (an Islamic holiday) next month, provided that the prisoner release work is completed first.
The biggest concern for Pakistan is the militants within Afghanistan, particularly those linked to the TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, and Lashkar-e-Islam, or the Balochistan Liberation Army, which launched a large-scale attack in southern Sindh Province and southwestern Balochistan in that month.
Multiple Pakistani military personnel were killed in clashes with militants in southwestern Balochistan province.
The TTP carried out one of their most horrific attacks in Pakistan in 2014 that killed 140 members of the Pakistani Army Academy.
Most of them were students and some were as young as five years old.
The report states that “foreign terrorist fighters operating in Afghanistan pose a threat to the security of two countries, with an estimated number between 6,000 and 6,500 people most of whom belong to al-Qaeda.”
HSBC denies setting up "traps" for Chinese company Huawei in an investigation in the US – RT Business News
HSBC, a London-based multinational bank, said via social media that the bank had not fabricated evidence against Huawei as reported by Chinese media, leading to the arrest of Huawei's chief financial officer in Canada.
A WeChat article that is no longer available states that banks claimed their actions did not prompt an investigation by the US into Huawei, the world’s largest supplier of telecommunications network equipment, as scrutiny of Huawei’s alleged violations of US sanctions against Iran had already begun well before the banks’ involvement, in late 2016.
HSBC stated on Saturday that it "has no hostile intent towards Huawei and there is nothing 'frame' Huawei," and added that it would never distort any facts about its clients.
In response to the US Department of Justice’s request for information, HSBC merely stated objective facts.
HSBC did not “fabricate” evidence or “cover up” facts.”
The platform has shielded this article and is indicating that it "violates regulations." The article content cannot be viewed currently.
After multiple Chinese media outlets, including state-backed newspapers, accused HSBC of colluding with Washington to set up traps for Huawei, HSBC quickly issued this clarification.
In December 2018, Canadian authorities arrested Huawei's CFO Meng Wanzhou at the request of US agencies, and for nearly a year and a half, the company has been a focal point in diplomatic news.
Ma Xiaohu was charged with fraud for allegedly using shell companies to violate sanctions against Iran and failing to disclose the relationship between Huawei and Iranian company Skycom to banks.
The new evidence cited by The Global Times shows that prior to this, HSBC was well aware of the relationship between the Chinese company and Skycom.
It is reported that the bank chose to collude with US authorities in order to evade more severe penalties imposed on it in 2012, when HSBC was forced to admit wrongdoing related to money laundering and sanctions violations, including restrictions on Iran.
At that time, the bank was ordered to pay around $1.9 billion in fines and needed to implement measures to prevent similar misconduct.
In an article on the matter, the newspaper wrote: “There is evidence that HSBC knew about this relationship clearly but deliberately sold its customers’ confidential business documents to美方 in order to have the US government overlook the British bank’s misconduct and evade the sanctions it should have faced under the Deferred Prosecution Agreement of 2012.”
This state-controlled Chinese newspaper adds in an open warning that, should any collusion between banks be proven, such entities may face "severe punishment" in China (one of HSBC’s key markets).
In recent weeks this well-known bank has come under repeated criticism.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting implementation of the new national security law in Hong Kong, which is its business headquarters and contributes about half of its profits there.
Hsbc is in a double-bind situation that leaves it at odds with both the West and East: Secretary of State Mike Pompeo calls it “kowtowing to the United States,” while China’s media call for its support as “latecomer.”
Angola: Pedalé Hospital Transferred to Health Ministry Oversight
On Friday this week, the President of the Republic, João Lourenço, decided to transfer custody of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital, which is under construction in Luanda, to the Ministry of Health.
In a statement, the Office of the President of the Republic stated that the aim was to implement the construction principles of the Government, expand the network of hospitals and ensure better access to medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document also states that, under the current exceptional circumstances, the measure enables the people of Angola to have a differentiated hospital of reference value, equipped with modern technology and precise instruments for the diagnosis and treatment of complex diseases.
The message emphasizes that the decision of the President of Angola also takes into account the growth of the country's population, which drives the huge demand for the most diverse and high-quality healthcare services.
The document also notes that this requirement is justified because hospitals encompass the treatment, teaching, research and intervention of complex diseases related to ophthalmology and maternal-infant medicine, and that policies and management in these areas should fall within the responsibility of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghamama -- Algeria lost a patriot and a steadfast fighter.
Algiers - The President of the Republic Abdelmadjid Tebboune expressed his condolences to Mujahed Mahmoud Guema, a member of the National People's Assembly who had passed away on Friday, and said that Algeria “has lost a patriot and a fighter who has devoted himself to the cause of the people and the motherland”.
President Tebboune wrote in his condolences: "Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama has left us. Algeria has lost a patriot, a warrior who devoted himself to the nation and people's cause, whose determination never wavered from the moment he picked up arms, fighting alongside the valiant mujahedeen brothers until his last breath, which we can only listen to God’s decree on this day of our grief."
"Late Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace, came from a special generation of believers who sacrificed themselves for their country as patriotic fighters."
"In 1960, they joined the Tahart training camp of Wilaya VI at the height of their lives, joining the ranks of the revolution and freeing Algeria from the claws of inferior colonialism, sacrificing their lives."
“The late leader, with a spirit of combat and commitment to building a united and prosperous Algeria, is known by his name.”
The President added that Mahmoud Guemama, a highly respected mediator in the far south, once the spark of discord was erased, was there to help his fellow countrymen reach a reconciliation.
“These qualities and values enabled Mahmoud Guema to win the respect and trust of the Tamanrasset people by electing him as a representative of the people for several terms in the National People's Assembly, an ambassador and defender of the concerns and wishes of the people of the region and a voice for all the Algerian people.”
No eulogy could do justice to the tribute of Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace.
"Today, he left us at the most critical moment when Algeria needs him," said the President.
Finally, President Tebboune said: "I pray to Almighty Allah that the deceased may be bathed in the brightness of your holy mercy, in an immaculate paradise, to welcome him with the shuhadaa and the sunni".
I pray that true God will bestow upon his family and loved ones patience and comfort... Only you have infinite power."
Coronavirus Travel Guide: Countries Restricting Entry for Bulgarian Passengers
Many countries are implementing travel restrictions. From this week on, the Czech Republic wants to test Bulgarian seasonal workers for COVID-19. Denmark stipulates that only Bulgarian citizens who have registered their address in its territory, hold an employment contract or a business travel document can enter the country.
Starting today, people who have visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days will be subject to mandatory quarantine in Italy. Finland only accepts travellers who have legitimate reasons to enter.
Austria requires mandatory quarantine at home even if PCR test results are negative. Germany also implements the same policy for arrivals. For the next 14 days, entry is not permitted to southern Romania without sufficient reason. Greece has taken stricter measures on its land borders.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that from Tuesday, people flying from Bulgaria to Greece will be required to show negative proof of the PCR test for the novel coronavirus to the Greek border authorities, which must be completed within 72 hours of entering their neighbours in southern Greece.
They also announced that travellers must have a corresponding test certificate to be allowed into the country. The certificate must be in English with the corresponding three names and numbers on the traveller's passport or identity card. If it does not meet the above requirements, the authorities of the southern neighbouring countries will not accept it.
Passengers need to have RT-PCR tests conducted at the laboratory by collecting nasopharyngeal or oropharyngeal secretions through extraction. Accepted test results include those obtained from national reference laboratories, national health laboratories, or private labs that have been accredited by relevant national certification bodies (not necessarily specifically for COVID-19), after undergoing a test for COVID-19.
The measure does not apply to Greek citizens, citizens residing in Greece and travellers with significant professional reasons.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that passengers arriving in Greece on Tuesday, July 28, and Wednesday, July 29, who did not get tested before their departure will undergo testing upon arrival by Greek authorities.
The following requirements remain valid: Passengers travelling by plane to Greece must complete an electronic version of the Passenger Location Form (PLF) generating a two-dimensional code at least 24 hours in advance and submit the form to the Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For more information see travel.gov.gr
At present, there is no change in the requirements for moving to and from the land border between Bulgaria and Greece. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs explains that “passengers who cross the land border into Greece, regardless of their nationality, are required to produce a two-dimensional code at least 24 hours before arrival by filling in the electronic Passenger Positioning Form (PLF).
The closure of the Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda, and Makaza checkpoints has increased traffic flow near the border of Petrich. Crossing is not an issue, and there are no queues of trucks. Everyone is waiting to go sea from Greece. Passengers need to wait about an hour and a half to cross.
There are three checkpoints open on the Bulgarian-Greek border. Currently, "Ilinden - Exohi" and the segment in Ivaylovgrad are closed.
Dominic Cummings visits SAS base but is held hostage
The Prime Minister's chief adviser last week visited the base and experienced the taste of the hostage-taking snail.
The idea of holding Dominic Cummings as a hostage with a gun pointed at his head may not be the same for some of the harassed civil servants.
The hard-line Prime Minister's chief adviser visited the SAS headquarters in Stirling last week to experience a taste of the Middle East.
The Sunday Post revealed that part of his trip on Wednesday included a visit to the Counter-Terrorism Training House in the Counter-Terrorism Room, where elite soldiers honed their skills against terrorists.
He also toured the Hereford base under guidance, inspected the military equipment of the brigade, heard a briefing on recent operations, including efforts to clear Islamic State terrorists from Iraq.
The important visitors to the Stirling line-up were specially welcomed – try out “Inside Counter-Terrorism Training Centre”, where SAS trains close-quarter fighting in this two-storey building.
Princess Diana had visited here as well; in a training exercise in 1983, her hair had accidentally caught fire from the sparks of a thrown grenade.
It is understood that Mr Cummings arrived by helicopter and the base was named after Colonel David Stirling, the founder of the regiment.
He also visited the adjacent military training area of Petrilas, where several other top-secret units were stationed.
The visit was one of his visits to a highly classified British security facility that drew the attention of politicians and military leaders.
When news of his plans came out earlier this month, Tobias Ellwood, a member of the Conservative Party ' s Queen ' s Bench and Chairman of the House of Commons ' Special Committee on Defence, stated that members of Parliament should be given the same opportunity as ministers and civil servants to ask him about defence and security matters.
The Special Committee ' s requests for testimony from special advisers appointed by politicians are rare.
It is understood that senior SAS officials used Wednesday's activities to highlight the importance of maintaining the number of Royal Marines and Airborne troops.
It has reportedly been reported that these units, which have sent in large numbers of personnel to support Special Forces units, might be scaled down or merged in order to save costs.
According to a defence source, “SAS consists of 250 soldiers, more than half of whom were previously airborne, and the size of the Special Boat Squadron (SBS) is smaller and may not be sustainable without the support of the Royal Marine Corps”.
Mr Cummings was told that the units must be retained in their entirety.
Mr Cummings, who has complained that the military procurement pipeline continues to float billions of pounds, has recently attended meetings at MI5 and MI6 headquarters, and plans to visit the SBS headquarters in Poole, Dorsetshire, as well as the national top-secret laboratory in Boulton Down, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of Defence did not respond to a request for comment last night.
"Sunday Morning" Dayshow: Honoring Rodgers and Hart
It marks the centenary of the debut performance of one of Broadway's most legendary songwriting duos, Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart.
To honor these two songwriters, Broadway stars Jelani Alladin (of "Frozen" and "Godzilla") and Christiani Pitts (of "The King's Speech" and "The Four Kings") as well as dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (of "I Love Mike" and "Shuffle Along"), have performed a modern style version of "The Lovers," specially recorded for viewers of "Sunday Morning."
Bassist Russell Hall performs alongside drummer Bryan Carter, who provides the musical direction and arrangement for the show.
The California wildfires in Lassen County are under control.
Lassen County, northeast of Sacramento in California’s San Joaquin Valley, is currently battling two wildfires that have consumed over 12,000 hectares.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection said Saturday morning that the "Gold" Fire in the south of Arden Hills had been brought under control to only about 40 percent, with many houses, barns, and ranches still threatened by the wildfire, which could have a significant impact on local agriculture, livestock production, and timber operations.
The fire has been burning for four days and has destroyed about 849.8 hectares of land and seven buildings, with two firefighters injured and taken to the hospital.
Firefighters use limited conditions at night to activate and reinforce fire lines.
But according to weather forecasts, it was high winds and low humidity that day.
The Hog wildfire that broke out in the western part of Susanville has destroyed two buildings and about 4,047 hectares (over 10,026 acres) of land.
According to the California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection, the fire has been brought under control with firefighters working all night and tightening fire lines.
As of Saturday morning, the fire had been burning for six days and 45 percent of it was under control.
Be wary of those charming gardening "tricks" that seem harmless at first glance.
To plant enthusiasts this might be an embarrassing thing indeed, but I am quite addicted to garden tricks.
On the bright side, I think I am an open-minded or somewhat irrationally optimistic person, and many things in the plant world are often not intuitively judged.
But I am not ashamed to admit that I have fallen prey to such horticultural tricks time and again.
While I firmly believe in the value of trying new things, if I am not keen to open the “hell mode”, I can avoid some of the most common gardening tricks I have encountered below.
First of all, it's probably the biggest monkey in the area that grabs tomatoes with potatoes.
Transplanting tomatoes onto potatoes was hailed as a revolutionary breakthrough, though in fact it is an old trick that was attempted back in the 1920s.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family and have roughly similar internal “pipes,” so they could indeed be cross-bred for enhanced magic.
But the problem is that creating resource-intensive structures like fruits and root crops requires a large amount of energy for metabolism, and grafted plants need to satisfy the energy needs of both crops simultaneously, which limits the allocation of limited energy.
This means that the yields of these grafted plants are very low, quite different from what you see in the promotional pictures.
Besides, different crops ripen at different times, so potatoes have to be harvested before tomatoes turn ripe.
At the same time, the propaganda picture implies a lot of confusing tricks.
I've seen roses with PS in some catalogues, and some fruit trees produce incredible amounts of fruit.
Some merchants even “borrow” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim them to be part of their test bases on online shopping websites.
These unscrupulous merchants' practices make you have to view the promotional images, especially those from the internet, with a skeptical eye.
If it looks too good to be true, it probably is.
Speaking of blue roses, the blue is relatively rare in the plant world, so if you see a new variety of fancy broomstones, orchids or cactus, with a high concentration of “sterilizers”, that must have been sprayed or dyed.
If the supplier had clearly stated the situation, it would have been nothing, but they rarely would have done so.
Blue butterflies will turn back to their original white color, but after being coated with a layer of refractive dye, they will soon fade.
Finally, I must expose the shame of bonsai kits that promise miniature trees and worse bonsai seeds.
Bonsai is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Kiosks are typically mature shrubs that are then shaped and guide new growth directions with wires.
I know that I will continue to be fascinated by these horticultural gimmicks and that this is part of experimentation’s fun.
But avoiding these issues, you may have the chance to purchase an item that matches what the seller displays.
Do you need to quarantine in Spain?
No need, but if you return from Spain after midnight tonight, you must quarantine yourself at home for 14 days.
UK Justice Secretary Humza Yousaf said: "We decided earlier this week not to enforce quarantine measures for those returning from Spain because data shows an improvement in the spread of the virus."
But clearly, the latest data warrants our concern, which forces us to overturn the original decision.
We know that this would be disappointing. But we have made it clear that we will closely monitor the epidemic in all countries and that if the virus re-emerges in one country, we may ask that it be removed from the list of non-segregation measures.”
"The virus is still rampant and deadly. Our first priority is to contain the virus, prevent its spread and protect public health."
The government has not yet announced regulations regarding British citizens traveling to Spain, but you should check local rules in your area if you plan on visiting Spain.
Spanish emergency health coordinator MarÃa Jose Sierra said:
"There is a major epidemic here. This might be the second wave. We need to pay attention to what happens in the next few weeks."
Pakistan team Imam-Ul-Haq injured during warm-up can still participate
Pakistan and England will face each other in three Tests and three T20Is.
Imam-ul-Haq of Pakistan’s cricket team is to be considered fit after he was struck in the left hand during an intramural warm-up match at Derby.
The Pakistan Cricket Board (PCB) stated in a release: "The left-handed cricketer took part in a warm-up match before the game on the second day."
Imam was hit on the left hand while playing against White team PCB's Naseem Shah at Day One of the competition.
On Friday evening, when Imam was injured and forced to retire after being struck in the left hand by a ball thrown by Shah, he participated in an early morning warm-up match but will return to the field on the third day.
Over 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have tested positive for COVID-19.
Officials from the government department of the Tiruchirappalli municipal corporation in Tamil Nadu and one state-owned bank said that at least 38 employees of a main branch of the bank located in Tiruchirappalli had tested positive for coronavirus.
Officials say they have advised customers who visited the branch to voluntarily participate in virus testing.
Previously, a senior official of the branch had other complications and died of pneumonia.
Officials said that recently, they conducted a broad medical screening at the branch, which led to the discovery of these positive cases.
Bank officials say they have completed the disinfection and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officials are in touch with customers who visited the bank.
Tamil Nadu is the second largest state in India with the highest number of confirmed cases in Maharashtra, with a total of 206,737 confirmed cases reported by Tamil Nadu as of Saturday.
India's Prime Minister will attend the groundbreaking ceremony for a temple built on a disputed site.
New Delhi (AP) – A trustee overseeing temple construction said Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi plans to attend next month the groundbreaking ceremony for a Hindu temple being built on a disputed site in northern India, where a mosque that dated back to the 16th century was destroyed by Hindu extremists in 1992.
The ceremony is scheduled to take place on August 5 and organizers say that this day is auspicious for Hindus but also marks one year since the Indian Parliament revoked the semi-autonomous status of Jammu and Kashmir, which has a majority Muslim population.
Both supporters and opponents of Modi's Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) cannot miss this symbolic day, which in its manifesto decades ago pledged to seize the autonomy of Kashmir and build a temple dedicated to the Hindu god Ram on the original site of a Mughal-era mosque in Ayodhya, Northeast India, believed by believers to be the birthplace of Ram.
The coronavirus is still spreading across India, which has reportedly become the third country with the highest number of cases globally, given that the ceremony will be broadcast live on national television, limiting the number of people and spectators attending the ceremony, according to Vishwa Hindu Parishad, a Hindu nationalist group allied with the Bharatiya Janata Party.
The organization's spokesperson, Vinod Bansal, said at a press conference on Saturday that the temple will become "a perpetual center for social harmony, national unity and integration, awakening of Hinduism."
Following the overwhelming victory of the Bharatiya Janata Party in the election, a century-long dispute over the site was resolved last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of the temple trust, saying that he would instead allocate five acres of land to Muslim petitioners.
Hindu fanatics have long claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of the existing temple in the ancient city of Ayutthaya.
In 1992, riots following the demolition of the mosque in December triggered communal violence in which some 2,000 people, mostly Muslims, were killed.
Meanwhile, the special court is still hearing the case of the mosque demolition.
An architect from Ahmedabad, Gujarat, India, hometown of Modi, proposes building a tower-shaped sandstone structure temple that is 161 feet (49 meters) high with five domes.
